   ],
   "l": "Favour and disgrace — like a start;\nprize great trouble like your own body.\nWhat is meant by 'favour and disgrace — like a start'?\nFavour is the lower place:\nget it — like a start,\nlose it — like a start.\nThis is what is meant by 'favour and disgrace — like a start'.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Favour and disgrace alike come as a jolt; prize great calamity as you prize your own body. What is meant by 'favour and disgrace come as a jolt'? Favour puts you underneath: you are jolted to receive it and jolted to lose it. That is what is meant by 'favour and disgrace come as a jolt'.",
   "n": "Four characters, no subject and no verb 'to be': 寵辱若驚 is 'favour | disgrace | like | a start'. 驚 is the word for a horse shying, so the sense is a jolt rather than fear. The chapter then does something it does once more below: it puts its own question (何謂…？) and answers it (是謂…) — a small catechism, and a shape to recognise. The sharp part is the answer: 寵為下, 'favour is the lower place'. To be somebody's favourite is to be somebody's inferior, which is why being raised up jolts a man exactly as being cast down does. The second clause, 貴大患若身, is compressed to the point of dispute; the chapter's own answer settles it — the great trouble is precisely having a body — but the line is divided other ways by other readers.",
   "p": true
  },
