   "l": "That whereby I have great trouble —\nit is because I have a body,",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The reason I have great trouble is that I have a body;",
   "n": "所以…者 is a frame: 所以 'that by which' opens it, 者 closes it, and everything between becomes one noun — 'the-reason-I-have-great-trouble'. Beware the modern trap: 所以 in today's Mandarin means 'therefore', but classically it is the how or the why of a thing, never the 'so' that follows from it. And 為 has changed both job and reading within four lines: 寵為下 was wéi, 'is'; here it is wèi, 'because of'."
