   "l": "what trouble have I?\nTherefore: prize taking your own person as all-under-heaven —\nthen all-under-heaven may be lodged with you;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "what trouble have I then? So: prize your own person above all under heaven, and all under heaven may be left in your keeping;",
   "n": "以…為… is the standard 'take A as B' frame: 以身為天下, 'take one's own person as all under heaven'. What that amounts to is genuinely argued — either value your person as highly as the world, or value it above the world. The English here goes the second way because Wang Bi does: 無以易其身, 'there is nothing he would exchange his own person for' — and the man who is not for sale is exactly the man the world can be handed to. The gentler reading, person and world weighed equally, is a real one and some readers prefer it. Watch 若 too: earlier in the chapter it was 'like'; here it is 'then, in that case'. One character, two jobs, and no ending to tell them apart — only position."
  },
