   "l": "Cherish taking your own person as all-under-heaven —\nthen all-under-heaven may be entrusted to you.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "cherish your own person above all under heaven, and all under heaven may be handed into your care.",
   "n": "The couplet says itself over with two changes: 貴 'prize' becomes 愛 'cherish', and 寄 becomes 託. 寄 is what you do with a parcel you mean to collect again; 託 is putting a thing into a man's hands and leaving it there. Near-synonymous doubling of this kind is how the book makes a point twice without repeating itself, and it is everywhere. 愛 here is less warm feeling than sparing use: to be careful of a thing, to grudge spending it."
